# =============================================================================
# Lv-00 Axiom Package: Graph Theory
# =============================================================================
#
# Corresponding Mathematical Theory:
#   Graph theory — the study of graphs as mathematical structures modeling
#   pairwise relations between objects. A graph G = (V, E) consists of a set
#   V of vertices (nodes) and a set E of edges (links), where each edge is
#   an unordered pair {u, v} of distinct vertices (for simple graphs).
#
# References:
#   - Euler, L. (1736). "Solutio problematis ad geometriam situs pertinentis."
#     Commentarii Academiae Scientiarum Imperialis Petropolitanae, 8: 128-140.
#     https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seven_Bridges_of_K%C3%B6nigsberg
#   - Diestel, R. (2017). "Graph Theory" (5th ed.). Springer.
#     Graduate Texts in Mathematics 173. ISBN 978-3-662-53621-6.
#   - Bondy, J.A. and Murty, U.S.R. (2008). "Graph Theory." Springer.
#     Graduate Texts in Mathematics 244. ISBN 978-1-84628-969-9.
#   - Wikipedia: Graph theory
#     https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Graph_theory
#   - Wikipedia: Graph (discrete mathematics)
#     https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Graph_(discrete_mathematics)
#   - nLab: graph
#     https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/graph
#   - West, D.B. (2001). "Introduction to Graph Theory" (2nd ed.).
#     Prentice Hall. ISBN 0-13-014400-2.
#   - Bollobás, B. (1998). "Modern Graph Theory." Springer.
#     Graduate Texts in Mathematics 184. ISBN 978-0-387-98488-9.
#   - Harary, F. (1969). "Graph Theory." Addison-Wesley.
#     ISBN 0-201-02787-9.
#
# Formal Language:
#   Primitive notions:
#     - Vertex (element of set V, the vertex set)
#     - Edge (element of set E, the edge set)
#     - Incidence (relation between edges and their endpoint vertices)
#     - Adjacency (symmetric relation between vertices sharing an edge)
#
#   A graph G = (V, E) where:
#     - V is a finite set of vertices
#     - E ⊆ {{u,v} : u, v ∈ V, u ≠ v} for simple undirected graphs
#     - Each edge e ∈ E is incident to exactly two vertices (its endpoints)
#
# Core Axioms:
#   I.   Graph Definition (G1-G3):
#        G1: V is a finite set (vertex set)
#        G2: E is a set of 2-element subsets of V (edge set, simple graph)
#        G3: Every edge is incident to exactly two distinct vertices
#
#   II.  Connectivity (G4-G5):
#        G4: A path is a sequence of distinct vertices v₀,v₁,...,vₖ
#            where {vᵢ,vᵢ₊₁} ∈ E for all 0 ≤ i < k
#        G5: G is connected iff for every u, v ∈ V, there exists a path from u to v
#
#   III. Graph Operations (G6-G10):
#        G6: Subgraph: G' = (V', E') where V' ⊆ V and E' ⊆ E ∩ (V' × V')
#        G7: Complement: Ḡ = (V, V×V \ E)
#        G8: Union: G₁ ∪ G₂ = (V₁ ∪ V₂, E₁ ∪ E₂)
#        G9: Intersection: G₁ ∩ G₂ = (V₁ ∩ V₂, E₁ ∩ E₂)
#        G10: Vertex deletion: G - v = (V \ {v}, E \ {e ∈ E : v ∈ e})
#
#   IV.  Special Graph Classes (G11-G16):
#        G11: Complete graph Kₙ: E = {{u,v} : u, v ∈ V, u ≠ v}
#        G12: Bipartite graph: V = V₁ ∪ V₂, V₁ ∩ V₂ = ∅, E ⊆ V₁ × V₂
#        G13: Tree: connected acyclic graph (|E| = |V| - 1)
#        G14: Forest: acyclic graph (disjoint union of trees)
#        G15: Cycle graph Cₙ: connected 2-regular graph on n vertices
#        G16: Planar graph: can be embedded in the plane without edge crossings
#
#   V.   Fundamental Theorems (G17-G20):
#        G17: Handshaking Lemma: Σ deg(v) = 2|E|
#        G18: Euler's Formula (planar): |V| - |E| + |F| = 2
#        G19: König's Theorem: α'(G) = τ(G) for bipartite G
#              (matching number = vertex cover number)
#        G20: Menger's Theorem: κ(u,v) = λ(u,v) for any u, v ∈ V
#              (minimum vertex cut = maximum internally vertex-disjoint paths)
#
# Known Undecidable / NP-Complete Problems:
#   - Graph 3-coloring: NP-complete (reduction from 3-SAT)
#   - Hamiltonian cycle: NP-complete (Karp 1972)
#   - Subgraph isomorphism: NP-complete
#   - Graph isomorphism: not known to be in P or NP-complete (Babai 2015: quasi-polynomial)
#   - Treewidth computation: NP-complete
#   - Graph minor containment: O(n³) by Robertson-Seymour, but fixed-parameter
#   - Clique decision: NP-complete
#   - Independent set: NP-complete
#   - Vertex cover: NP-complete
#   - Dominating set: NP-complete
#   - Graph coloring (k-coloring for k ≥ 3): NP-complete
#   - Steiner tree: NP-complete
#   - Feedback vertex set: NP-complete
#
# Relationship to Other Theories:
#   - Graph theory is a subfield of discrete mathematics / combinatorics
#   - Trees are intimately connected to order theory (partial orders)
#   - Planar graphs connect to topology (Euler characteristic, embeddings)
#   - Graph coloring connects to algebra (chromatic polynomial, group theory)
#   - Spectral graph theory connects to linear algebra (adjacency matrix eigenvalues)
#   - Category theory: graphs are presheaves on the category • ⇉ •
#   - Graph algorithms connect to computational complexity theory
#
# Author: Lv-00 Axiom Package System (AI-Discovered)
# Date: 2026-05-24
# Version: 1.0.0
# =============================================================================

axiom "graph_theory" "1.0.0" {

    # =================================================================
    # Constraint Templates
    # =================================================================
    # Each template represents a fundamental axiom, operation, property,
    # or derived construction in graph theory.
    #
    # Parameter semantics:
    #   Params = vertices (elements of V) or edges (elements of E)
    #   Unless otherwise noted, all params are vertices.
    # =================================================================

    # --- Group I: Core Graph Definition (3 axioms) ---

    # Axiom G1: Vertex Set
    #   V is a finite set of vertices.
    #   The vertex set is the carrier of the graph structure.
    # Params: (none) -> vertex set V
    template "vertex_set" 0

    # Axiom G2: Edge Set
    #   E ⊆ {{u,v} : u, v ∈ V, u ≠ v} is a set of unordered pairs.
    #   For simple graphs, no loops (u = v) and no multiple edges.
    # Params: vertex u, vertex v -> edge {u, v}
    template "edge_set" 2

    # Axiom G3: Incidence
    #   Every edge e = {u, v} is incident to exactly two distinct
    #   vertices u and v. The incidence relation is symmetric.
    # Params: edge e -> endpoints (u, v)
    template "incidence" 1

    # --- Group II: Adjacency & Degree (5 templates) ---

    # Adjacency Relation
    #   Two vertices u and v are adjacent iff {u, v} ∈ E.
    #   Adjacency is symmetric and irreflexive (for simple graphs).
    # Params: vertex u, vertex v -> check result
    template "adjacency" 2

    # Vertex Degree
    #   deg(v) = |{u ∈ V : {u, v} ∈ E}|
    #   The degree of a vertex is the number of edges incident to it.
    # Params: vertex v -> degree deg(v)
    template "vertex_degree" 1

    # Handshaking Lemma (G17)
    #   Σ_{v ∈ V} deg(v) = 2|E|
    #   The sum of all vertex degrees equals twice the number of edges.
    # Params: (none) -> equation
    template "handshaking_lemma" 0

    # Maximum Degree
    #   Δ(G) = max{deg(v) : v ∈ V}
    # Params: (none) -> max degree Δ(G)
    template "maximum_degree" 0

    # Minimum Degree
    #   δ(G) = min{deg(v) : v ∈ V}
    # Params: (none) -> min degree δ(G)
    template "minimum_degree" 0

    # --- Group III: Paths, Walks, and Connectivity (8 templates) ---

    # Walk
    #   A walk is a sequence of vertices v₀, v₁, ..., vₖ where
    #   {vᵢ, vᵢ₊₁} ∈ E for all 0 ≤ i < k. Vertices may repeat.
    # Params: vertex v₀, vertex vₖ -> walk from v₀ to vₖ
    template "walk" 2

    # Path
    #   A path is a walk with all vertices distinct.
    # Params: vertex v₀, vertex vₖ -> path from v₀ to vₖ
    template "path" 2

    # Path Length
    #   The length of a path is the number of edges traversed.
    # Params: path p -> length (integer)
    template "path_length" 1

    # Distance (Shortest Path Length)
    #   d(u, v) = length of shortest path from u to v, or ∞ if none.
    # Params: vertex u, vertex v -> distance d(u, v)
    template "distance" 2

    # Connected Graph
    #   G is connected iff ∀u, v ∈ V, ∃ path from u to v.
    # Params: (none) -> check result
    template "connected" 0

    # Connected Component
    #   A maximal connected subgraph of G.
    # Params: vertex v -> connected component containing v
    template "connected_component" 1

    # Number of Connected Components
    #   c(G) = number of connected components of G.
    # Params: (none) -> count c(G)
    template "component_count" 0

    # Connectivity (Vertex)
    #   κ(G) = minimum number of vertices whose removal disconnects G.
    # Params: (none) -> vertex connectivity κ(G)
    template "vertex_connectivity" 0

    # --- Group IV: Cycles and Trees (8 templates) ---

    # Cycle
    #   A cycle is a closed path v₀, v₁, ..., vₖ = v₀ with k ≥ 3
    #   and all vertices distinct except v₀ = vₖ.
    # Params: vertex v₀ -> cycle containing v₀
    template "cycle" 1

    # Cycle Length (Girth)
    #   g(G) = length of the shortest cycle in G, or ∞ if acyclic.
    # Params: (none) -> girth g(G)
    template "girth" 0

    # Circumference
    #   The length of the longest cycle in G.
    # Params: (none) -> circumference
    template "circumference" 0

    # Acyclic Graph (Forest)
    #   G is acyclic iff G contains no cycles.
    # Params: (none) -> check result
    template "acyclic" 0

    # Tree
    #   A tree is a connected acyclic graph.
    #   Equivalently: |E| = |V| - 1 and connected.
    # Params: (none) -> check result
    template "tree" 0

    # Forest
    #   A forest is a disjoint union of trees (acyclic graph).
    #   Equivalently: |E| = |V| - c(G).
    # Params: (none) -> check result
    template "forest" 0

    # Spanning Tree
    #   A subgraph T ⊆ G that is a tree and V(T) = V(G).
    #   Every connected graph has at least one spanning tree.
    # Params: (none) -> spanning tree
    template "spanning_tree" 0

    # Leaf Vertex
    #   A vertex of degree 1. Every tree with |V| ≥ 2 has ≥ 2 leaves.
    # Params: vertex v -> check result
    template "leaf" 1

    # --- Group V: Graph Operations (12 templates) ---

    # Subgraph
    #   G' = (V', E') is a subgraph of G iff V' ⊆ V and E' ⊆ E.
    # Params: vertex subset V', edge subset E' -> subgraph G'
    template "subgraph" 2

    # Induced Subgraph
    #   G[S] = (S, {e ∈ E : e ⊆ S}) for S ⊆ V.
    # Params: vertex subset S -> induced subgraph G[S]
    template "induced_subgraph" 1

    # Complement Graph
    #   Ḡ = (V, {{u,v} : u, v ∈ V, u ≠ v} \ E).
    # Params: (none) -> complement graph Ḡ
    template "complement" 0

    # Graph Union
    #   G₁ ∪ G₂ = (V₁ ∪ V₂, E₁ ∪ E₂).
    # Params: graph G₁, graph G₂ -> union G₁ ∪ G₂
    template "graph_union" 2

    # Graph Intersection
    #   G₁ ∩ G₂ = (V₁ ∩ V₂, E₁ ∩ E₂).
    # Params: graph G₁, graph G₂ -> intersection G₁ ∩ G₂
    template "graph_intersection" 2

    # Vertex Deletion
    #   G - v = (V \ {v}, {e ∈ E : v ∉ e}).
    # Params: vertex v -> graph G - v
    template "vertex_deletion" 1

    # Edge Deletion
    #   G - e = (V, E \ {e}).
    # Params: edge e -> graph G - e
    template "edge_deletion" 1

    # Vertex Contraction
    #   G / e: contract edge e = {u,v} into a single vertex uv,
    #   merging all incident edges.
    # Params: edge e -> graph G / e
    template "edge_contraction" 1

    # Line Graph
    #   L(G): vertices of L(G) are edges of G; two vertices of L(G)
    #   are adjacent iff the corresponding edges of G share an endpoint.
    # Params: (none) -> line graph L(G)
    template "line_graph" 0

    # Cartesian Product of Graphs
    #   G □ H: V(G □ H) = V(G) × V(H); {(u₁,v₁),(u₂,v₂)} ∈ E(G □ H)
    #   iff u₁ = u₂ and {v₁,v₂} ∈ E(H), or v₁ = v₂ and {u₁,u₂} ∈ E(G).
    # Params: graph G, graph H -> product G □ H
    template "cartesian_product" 2

    # Graph Join
    #   G + H = (V(G) ∪ V(H), E(G) ∪ E(H) ∪ {{u,v} : u ∈ V(G), v ∈ V(H)}).
    # Params: graph G, graph H -> join G + H
    template "graph_join" 2

    # Disjoint Union
    #   G ⊔ H: the union of G and H with disjoint vertex sets.
    # Params: graph G, graph H -> disjoint union G ⊔ H
    template "disjoint_union" 2

    # --- Group VI: Special Graph Classes (10 templates) ---

    # Complete Graph Kₙ
    #   Kₙ = (V, {{u,v} : u, v ∈ V, u ≠ v}). Every pair of distinct
    #   vertices is connected by an edge. |E| = n(n-1)/2.
    # Params: integer n -> complete graph Kₙ
    template "complete_graph" 1

    # Empty Graph Ẽₙ
    #   Ẽₙ = (V, ∅). No edges. Complement of Kₙ.
    # Params: integer n -> empty graph Ẽₙ
    template "empty_graph" 1

    # Path Graph Pₙ
    #   Pₙ: a tree on n vertices with exactly 2 leaves.
    # Params: integer n -> path graph Pₙ
    template "path_graph" 1

    # Cycle Graph Cₙ
    #   Cₙ: a connected 2-regular graph on n vertices (n ≥ 3).
    # Params: integer n -> cycle graph Cₙ
    template "cycle_graph" 1

    # Bipartite Graph
    #   G is bipartite iff V can be partitioned into V₁, V₂ such that
    #   every edge has one endpoint in V₁ and one in V₂.
    #   Equivalently: G has no odd cycle.
    # Params: (none) -> check result
    template "bipartite" 0

    # Complete Bipartite Graph K_{m,n}
    #   K_{m,n}: bipartite graph with |V₁| = m, |V₂| = n, all possible
    #   edges between V₁ and V₂. |E| = m·n.
    # Params: integer m, integer n -> K_{m,n}
    template "complete_bipartite" 2

    # Regular Graph
    #   G is k-regular iff every vertex has degree k.
    # Params: integer k -> check result
    template "regular" 1

    # Planar Graph
    #   G is planar iff G can be embedded in the plane without edge crossings.
    #   By Kuratowski's theorem: G is planar iff it contains no subdivision
    #   of K₅ or K_{3,3}.
    # Params: (none) -> check result
    template "planar" 0

    # Eulerian Graph
    #   G is Eulerian iff G is connected and every vertex has even degree.
    #   Equivalently: G has a closed trail using every edge exactly once.
    # Params: (none) -> check result
    template "eulerian" 0

    # Hamiltonian Graph
    #   G is Hamiltonian iff G contains a cycle visiting every vertex
    #   exactly once. Deciding this is NP-complete.
    # Params: (none) -> check result
    template "hamiltonian" 0

    # --- Group VII: Matching and Covering (8 templates) ---

    # Matching
    #   A matching M ⊆ E is a set of pairwise non-adjacent edges
    #   (no two edges share a vertex).
    # Params: edge subset M -> check result (is matching)
    template "matching" 1

    # Maximum Matching
    #   A matching of maximum cardinality. ν(G) = |M_max|.
    # Params: (none) -> maximum matching M_max
    template "maximum_matching" 0

    # Perfect Matching
    #   A matching M where every vertex is incident to exactly one edge
    #   in M. Requires |V| to be even.
    # Params: (none) -> check result
    template "perfect_matching" 0

    # Vertex Cover
    #   A vertex cover C ⊆ V is a set of vertices such that every edge
    #   has at least one endpoint in C. τ(G) = |C_min|.
    # Params: vertex subset C -> check result (is vertex cover)
    template "vertex_cover" 1

    # Minimum Vertex Cover
    #   A vertex cover of minimum cardinality. τ(G) = |C_min|.
    # Params: (none) -> minimum vertex cover C_min
    template "minimum_vertex_cover" 0

    # Independent Set
    #   An independent set S ⊆ V is a set of pairwise non-adjacent vertices.
    #   α(G) = |S_max|. Note: S is independent iff V \ S is a vertex cover.
    # Params: vertex subset S -> check result (is independent set)
    template "independent_set" 1

    # Maximum Independent Set
    #   An independent set of maximum cardinality. α(G) = |S_max|.
    # Params: (none) -> maximum independent set S_max
    template "maximum_independent_set" 0

    # Dominating Set
    #   A dominating set D ⊆ V is such that every vertex in V \ D
    #   is adjacent to at least one vertex in D.
    # Params: vertex subset D -> check result (is dominating set)
    template "dominating_set" 1

    # --- Group VIII: Graph Coloring (6 templates) ---

    # Proper Vertex Coloring
    #   A function c: V → {1, 2, ..., k} such that c(u) ≠ c(v)
    #   whenever {u, v} ∈ E (adjacent vertices get different colors).
    # Params: integer k -> coloring function c
    template "vertex_coloring" 1

    # Chromatic Number
    #   χ(G) = minimum k such that G has a proper k-coloring.
    #   χ(G) = 1 iff G has no edges; χ(G) = 2 iff G is bipartite
    #   and has at least one edge.
    # Params: (none) -> chromatic number χ(G)
    template "chromatic_number" 0

    # Chromatic Polynomial
    #   P(G, k) = number of proper k-colorings of G.
    #   P(G, k) is a polynomial in k (Birkhoff 1912, Whitney 1932).
    # Params: integer k -> count P(G, k)
    template "chromatic_polynomial" 1

    # Edge Coloring
    #   A function c': E → {1, 2, ..., k} such that edges sharing
    #   a vertex get different colors. χ'(G) = Δ(G) or Δ(G) + 1
    #   (Vizing's theorem).
    # Params: integer k -> edge coloring function c'
    template "edge_coloring" 1

    # Chromatic Index
    #   χ'(G) = minimum k for a proper edge coloring.
    #   By Vizing's theorem: Δ(G) ≤ χ'(G) ≤ Δ(G) + 1.
    # Params: (none) -> chromatic index χ'(G)
    template "chromatic_index" 0

    # Clique
    #   A clique K ⊆ V is a set of pairwise adjacent vertices.
    #   ω(G) = size of the largest clique.
    # Params: vertex subset K -> check result (is clique)
    template "clique" 1

    # --- Group IX: Fundamental Theorems (5 templates) ---

    # Euler's Formula for Planar Graphs (G18)
    #   For a connected planar graph: |V| - |E| + |F| = 2,
    #   where F is the number of faces in any planar embedding.
    # Params: (none) -> equation
    template "euler_formula_planar" 0

    # König's Theorem (G19)
    #   For bipartite graphs: α'(G) = τ(G).
    #   The size of a maximum matching equals the size of a minimum
    #   vertex cover. This is equivalent to Hall's marriage theorem
    #   and the max-flow min-cut theorem for bipartite networks.
    # Params: (none) -> equation
    template "konigs_theorem" 0

    # Menger's Theorem (G20)
    #   For any two non-adjacent vertices u, v in G:
    #   κ(u,v) = λ(u,v) = maximum number of internally vertex-disjoint
    #   u-v paths. (Vertex version; edge version also exists.)
    # Params: vertex u, vertex v -> equation
    template "mengers_theorem" 2

    # Kuratowski's Theorem
    #   G is planar iff G contains no subdivision of K₅ or K_{3,3}.
    #   This is the fundamental characterization of planarity.
    # Params: (none) -> check result (planarity via Kuratowski)
    template "kuratowskis_theorem" 0

    # Four Color Theorem
    #   Every planar graph is 4-colorable: χ(G) ≤ 4 for planar G.
    #   Proved by Appel and Haken (1976), simplified by Robertson
    #   et al. (1997). One of the most famous theorems in mathematics.
    # Params: (none) -> bound χ(G) ≤ 4
    template "four_color_theorem" 0

    # --- Group X: Advanced Properties (5 templates) ---

    # Graph Isomorphism
    #   G ≅ H iff there exists a bijection f: V(G) → V(H) preserving
    #   adjacency: {u,v} ∈ E(G) ↔ {f(u),f(v)} ∈ E(H).
    #   GI is in NP but not known to be NP-complete or in P.
    #   Babai (2015): quasi-polynomial time algorithm.
    # Params: graph G, graph H -> check result
    template "graph_isomorphism" 2

    # Graph Automorphism
    #   An automorphism of G is an isomorphism from G to itself.
    #   Aut(G) forms a group under composition.
    # Params: (none) -> automorphism group Aut(G)
    template "graph_automorphism" 0

    # Treewidth
    #   The treewidth tw(G) measures how "tree-like" a graph is.
    #   Many NP-hard problems become polynomial on bounded-treewidth graphs
    #   (Courcelle's theorem). Computing treewidth is NP-complete.
    # Params: (none) -> treewidth tw(G)
    template "treewidth" 0

    # Graph Minor
    #   H is a minor of G iff H can be obtained from G by vertex/edge
    #   deletions and edge contractions. Robertson-Seymour theorem:
    #   every minor-closed family has a finite forbidden minor set.
    # Params: graph H -> check result (H is minor of G)
    template "graph_minor" 1

    # Spectral Properties (Adjacency Eigenvalues)
    #   The eigenvalues λ₁ ≥ λ₂ ≥ ... ≥ λₙ of the adjacency matrix A
    #   encode structural information: λ₁ ≥ 2|E|/|V| (average degree),
    #   G is bipartite iff λ₁ = -λₙ, G is connected iff λ₁ > λ₂.
    # Params: (none) -> eigenvalue spectrum
    template "spectral_properties" 0


    # =================================================================
    # Known Unconstructible / Undecidable Problems
    # =================================================================
    # Each entry documents a problem in graph theory known to be
    # NP-complete, NP-hard, or otherwise computationally intractable.
    # =================================================================

    # 1. Graph 3-Coloring
    # "Can the vertices of G be colored with 3 colors so that
    #  no two adjacent vertices share a color?"
    # NP-complete (reduction from 3-SAT; Stockmeyer 1973).
    unconstructible "graph_3_coloring" {
        reduces_to "NP_complete"
        dependency "vertex_coloring"
        dependency "chromatic_number"
        dependency "three_colorability"
        external_ref "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Graph_coloring#Computational_complexity"
        green_verified true
    }

    # 2. Hamiltonian Cycle Problem
    # "Does G contain a cycle that visits every vertex exactly once?"
    # NP-complete (Karp 1972, one of the original 21 NP-complete problems).
    unconstructible "hamiltonian_cycle" {
        reduces_to "NP_complete"
        dependency "cycle"
        dependency "hamiltonian"
        dependency "path"
        external_ref "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hamiltonian_path_problem"
        green_verified true
    }

    # 3. Subgraph Isomorphism
    # "Does G contain a subgraph isomorphic to H?"
    # NP-complete (generalizes clique detection).
    unconstructible "subgraph_isomorphism" {
        reduces_to "NP_complete"
        dependency "subgraph"
        dependency "graph_isomorphism"
        dependency "induced_subgraph"
        external_ref "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Subgraph_isomorphism_problem"
        green_verified true
    }

    # 4. Graph Isomorphism
    # "Are G and H isomorphic?"
    # Not known to be in P or NP-complete. In NP.
    # Babai (2015): quasi-polynomial time algorithm exp(O((log n)³)).
    unconstructible "graph_isomorphism_problem" {
        reduces_to "GI_complexity_class"
        dependency "graph_isomorphism"
        dependency "graph_automorphism"
        external_ref "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Graph_isomorphism_problem"
        green_verified true
    }

    # 5. Treewidth Computation
    # "What is the treewidth of G?"
    # NP-complete to compute (Arnborg, Corneil, Proskurowski 1987).
    # Deciding tw(G) ≤ k is NP-complete for general k, but
    # polynomial for fixed k (Bodlaender 1996: O(n) for fixed k).
    unconstructible "treewidth_computation" {
        reduces_to "NP_complete"
        dependency "treewidth"
        dependency "subgraph"
        dependency "vertex_deletion"
        external_ref "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Treewidth"
        green_verified true
    }

    # 6. Maximum Clique Problem
    # "What is the size of the largest clique in G?"
    # NP-complete (equivalent to maximum independent set via complement).
    unconstructible "maximum_clique" {
        reduces_to "NP_complete"
        dependency "clique"
        dependency "complement"
        dependency "independent_set"
        external_ref "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clique_problem"
        green_verified true
    }

    # 7. Maximum Independent Set
    # "What is the size of the largest independent set in G?"
    # NP-complete (Karp 1972). Equivalent to minimum vertex cover
    # via α(G) + τ(G) = |V|.
    unconstructible "maximum_independent_set_problem" {
        reduces_to "NP_complete"
        dependency "independent_set"
        dependency "maximum_independent_set"
        dependency "vertex_cover"
        external_ref "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Independent_set_(graph_theory)"
        green_verified true
    }

    # 8. Minimum Vertex Cover
    # "What is the size of the smallest vertex cover in G?"
    # NP-complete (Karp 1972). For bipartite graphs, polynomial
    # via König's theorem and maximum matching.
    unconstructible "minimum_vertex_cover_problem" {
        reduces_to "NP_complete"
        dependency "vertex_cover"
        dependency "minimum_vertex_cover"
        dependency "maximum_matching"
        external_ref "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vertex_cover"
        green_verified true
    }

    # 9. Minimum Dominating Set
    # "What is the size of the smallest dominating set in G?"
    # NP-complete (Karp 1972). Even APX-hard (cannot be approximated
    # within (1 - ε) ln n for any ε > 0 unless P = NP).
    unconstructible "minimum_dominating_set" {
        reduces_to "NP_complete"
        dependency "dominating_set"
        dependency "adjacency"
        external_ref "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dominating_set"
        green_verified true
    }

    # 10. Graph k-Coloring (for k ≥ 3)
    # "Can G be properly colored with k colors?"
    # NP-complete for k ≥ 3 (Garey, Johnson, Stockmeyer 1976).
    # Polynomial for k = 1 (check if E = ∅) and k = 2 (bipartiteness).
    unconstructible "graph_k_coloring" {
        reduces_to "NP_complete"
        dependency "vertex_coloring"
        dependency "chromatic_number"
        dependency "bipartite"
        external_ref "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Graph_coloring"
        green_verified true
    }

    # 11. Steiner Tree Problem
    # "Given a weighted graph and a set of terminals, find the
    #  minimum-weight tree connecting all terminals."
    # NP-complete (Karp 1972). Even APX-hard.
    unconstructible "steiner_tree" {
        reduces_to "NP_complete"
        dependency "spanning_tree"
        dependency "tree"
        dependency "path"
        external_ref "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Steiner_tree_problem"
        green_verified true
    }

    # 12. Feedback Vertex Set
    # "What is the minimum number of vertices whose removal makes
    #  G acyclic?"
    # NP-complete (Karp 1972).
    unconstructible "feedback_vertex_set" {
        reduces_to "NP_complete"
        dependency "acyclic"
        dependency "cycle"
        dependency "vertex_deletion"
        external_ref "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Feedback_vertex_set"
        green_verified true
    }

    # 13. Graph Homomorphism
    # "Does there exist a homomorphism from G to H?"
    # NP-complete in general. The k-coloring problem is the special
    # case of homomorphism to Kₖ.
    unconstructible "graph_homomorphism" {
        reduces_to "NP_complete"
        dependency "vertex_coloring"
        dependency "complete_graph"
        dependency "adjacency"
        external_ref "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Graph_homomorphism"
        green_verified true
    }

    # 14. Bandwidth Minimization
    # "Find a labeling f: V → {1,...,n} minimizing max|f(u)-f(v)|
    #  over all edges {u,v} ∈ E."
    # NP-complete (Papadimitriou 1976).
    unconstructible "bandwidth_minimization" {
        reduces_to "NP_complete"
        dependency "adjacency"
        dependency "path_length"
        external_ref "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bandwidth_(graph_theory)"
        green_verified true
    }


    # =================================================================
    # Bottom Geometry & Logical Framework
    # =================================================================
    #
    # bottom_geometry: "graph_incidence_structure"
    #   ⊥ (contradiction) in graph theory is geometrically represented
    #   as the empty graph (∅, ∅) — the graph with no vertices and no
    #   edges. This is the unique minimal element in the lattice of
    #   (finite) graphs ordered by the subgraph relation. A contradictory
    #   statement in graph theory corresponds to asserting the existence
    #   of a graph property that cannot be realized by any graph.
    #
    # negation_encoding: "classical_edge_complement"
    #   ¬φ(G) is encoded via the complement operation on the graph
    #   universe: the negation of a graph property φ is the class of
    #   graphs satisfying the complementary property. Since graph theory
    #   uses classical logic (e.g., in proofs of theorems like the
    #   Four Color Theorem), negation satisfies the law of excluded
    #   middle. The complement graph Ḡ provides a geometric encoding
    #   of certain negated adjacency properties.
    #
    # contradiction_behavior: "explosion_principle"
    #   Graph theory uses classical logic with the principle of explosion:
    #   from a contradiction (e.g., asserting both that G is bipartite
    #   and that G contains an odd cycle), any conclusion follows.
    #   This is standard in all of classical mathematics.
    # =================================================================

    bottom_geometry "graph_incidence_structure"
    negation_encoding "classical_edge_complement"
    contradiction_behavior "explosion_principle"
}
